HOWTO:

mkvirtualenv wikiwalker -a "$PWD" -p /opt/local/bin/python3.14t
pip install -e ".[dev]"
pytest

python walker.py --help

Each scenario (test, read-only, crawl) has its own contextual help:

  python walker.py crawl --help

(test and read-only are testing scenarios. read-only requires data_cli to download/import the data)


Examples:
  ┌─────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │           command           │                                   shows                                    │
  ├─────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ walker.py test -v L0 L3_2   │ shortcut found at depth 2, not 3                                           │
  ├─────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ walker.py test -v L0 Orphan │ exhausts the graph: 40 pages, batches of 3/10/26, Red_Link 404 -> dead end │
  ├─────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ walker.py test -v L3_00 L0  │ leaf can't reach the root - no path, but complete                          │
  ├─────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ walker.py test Red_Link L0  │ red-link source: refused before any walk                                   │
  └─────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘


cargo install wikiparse-rs      # imports the simple.wikipedia.org dump faster
python3 data_cli.py simplewiki  # builds simplewiki.db (approx. 1.8GB)

  python3 walker.py read-only Bristol Cheese
  python3 walker.py read-only April Nigeria
  python3 walker.py read-only "South West England" "Walton Cardiff"


Crawling live Wikipedia, one request per second by default:

  python3 data_cli.py empty --wiki wikipedia-us
  python3 walker.py crawl Bristol England
  python3 walker.py crawl -v Bristol England --max-rps 0.5 --max-concurrency 3


NOTEs / trade offs:

- "eventual consistency": never a "consistent" snapshot

- html.parser.HTMLParser subclass, hrefs only │ 3-5ms vs BeautifulSoup + html.parser │ 50-100ms
    > not building a full DOM to cap cpu load

    parser reliability:
    4. html.parser is lenient, not spec-compliant. On malformed markup it can skip tags lxml would catch.
    5. JS-loaded content. Anything not in the served HTML is invisible. Collapsed navboxes are fine (present, just hidden);
    > html parsing is not always an "exact science". Stick to html.parser for simplicity of the context
    > filtering out category pages bc they are giant hubs ("article links" from pdf)
        > by section id="mw-content-text", and by name prefix within the content div

- wikiparse-rs for sql->sqlite // wikiwalk / wiki-graph — full solutions, avoid
    - did not look at wikiwalk OO design to build my own interpretation

- production-ready, so the progress is stored on db
    - if a link is in a page on db but not fetched yet, fetch+process
        if it's a red link, do not retry for 24 hours

        Simple walking, no lookahead within batch/chunk, or across chunks
        All links we do not have are fetched in background, starting at the beginning of the chunk
            The first one not yet fetched/processed blocks the loop.

- db + Walker supports red links
    - NOTE: red links vs transient errors TBD

- BatchLinks.drain(): if looking for shortest path first, it should be cancelled

- concurrency 10 is against wikipedia policies = might ban







  12. Whether extract_links works on current en.wikipedia rendering. Only the sample wiki and simplewiki dumps have been exercised.

  7. Redirect resolution emits duplicate edges — inflates link_count(), would skew most-frequent counts.
  11. Whether 1 req/s clears Wikimedia's limits for /wiki/ HTML — it's from their general guidance, not a published number.


